# The writers who recorded early Wales (November 2026): full pages for Gerald of Wales, Nennius, Asser, Geoffrey of
# Monmouth and Rhygyfarch, new pages for the other chroniclers, biographers and scribes, and the long read that ties
# them together (story-writers.html).
# Asked for by Ifan, 12 November 2026: "make a page on Giraldus Cambrensis (Gerald of Wales) and all the other
# authors, Nennius etc".
#
# House rules (EDITORIAL.md): proud; nothing negative about the people of Welsh history; Arthur is a king, and Welsh
# tradition holds that he was Athrwys ap Meurig; the Welsh are Britons and Cymry, never "Celts"; kings are kings.
# Gildas has his own page from the saints thread (src/people2a.py, src/sixth_century.py): this file only links to it.
#
# What is here:
#   LONG     slug -> (English paragraphs, Welsh paragraphs): full texts for people already on the site.
#   PEOPLE   new people, in the people2a.py format (people2.py adds them to the site).
#   GROUPS   the "Writers who recorded early Wales" block on each of their pages (build.py, writers_circle()).
#   LINKS    the pages that go with the block.
#   STORY    the long read story-writers.html (features.py adds it to the long reads).
#   REFS     its reading list (build.py merges it into refs.REFS).
#   CITES    the sources beside the claims used here (cites.py adds them to its list).
#
# Paragraph codes (see prose() in build.py): "## Heading", "> Quote | Source", "|| Left | Right" (a fact table).
#
# Sources used and checked (November 2026):
#   Gerald of Wales, The Itinerary through Wales and The Description of Wales, tr. Sir Richard Colt Hoare (Project
#   Gutenberg 1148 and 1092): Manorbier "the sweetest spot in Wales" (Introduction); Gerald first to take the cross at
#   Radnor and Rhys ap Gruffudd meeting them there (Itin. I.1); the birds of the lake of Brycheiniog proclaiming
#   Gruffudd ap Rhys (Itin. I.2); singing in parts (Desc. I.13); hospitality and the harp, "No one of this nation ever
#   begs" (Desc. I.10); boldness of speech before princes (Desc. I.15); "The Welsh esteem noble birth" (Desc. I.17);
#   "If, therefore, they would be inseparable, they would become insuperable" and the old man of Pencader (Desc. II.10).
#   Historia Brittonum, tr. J. A. Giles (Project Gutenberg 1972): the five poets; "the magnanimous Arthur, with all the
#   kings and military force of Britain"; "the fourth year of king Mermenus"; "disciple of St. Elbotus".
#   Wikipedia, Walter Map ("marchio sum Walensibus"; archdeacon of Oxford 1196; the Prose Lancelot names "Gautier Map")
#   and Caradoc of Llancarfan; the Dictionary of Welsh Biography; National Library of Wales, "Chronicle of the Princes".
from people2a import P

LONG = {}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Gerald of Wales

LONG["gerallt-gymro"] = (
 ["Gerald of Wales, Gerallt Gymro, brought twelfth-century Wales to life as no other writer has. In 1188 he rode round the whole country, and the two books he wrote about it, The Journey through Wales and The Description of Wales, are still read and loved more than eight hundred years later. He spent the best years of his life fighting to make St Davids the seat of an archbishop for Wales, free of Canterbury. He was proud of the blood of the princes of Wales in his veins, and Wales has been proud of him ever since.",
  "|| Born | About 1146, at Manorbier Castle, Pembrokeshire",
  "|| Father | William de Barri, knight of Manorbier",
  "|| Mother | Angharad, daughter of Nest ferch Rhys of Deheubarth",
  "|| His line | Grandson of Nest, great-grandson of Rhys ap Tewdwr, King of Deheubarth, and kinsman of the Lord Rhys",
  "|| Offices | Archdeacon of Brecon from 1175; royal clerk to King Henry II from 1184",
  "|| Books | About twenty, among them The Journey through Wales (1191) and The Description of Wales (1194)",
  "|| Died | About 1223",
  "## A boy of Manorbier",
  "Gerald was born in the castle of Manorbier, on the coast of Pembrokeshire, and he never forgot it. Dyfed, he wrote, was the fairest part of Wales, Pembroke the fairest part of Dyfed, and Manorbier the fairest part of Pembroke, so that Manorbier was “the sweetest spot in Wales”.[[cite:gerald-itin]] His father was a Norman knight, William de Barri. His mother, Angharad, was the daughter of Nest, the most famous princess of Wales, and through her Gerald was descended from Rhys ap Tewdwr, the last King of Deheubarth before the Normans came. He called himself by the name the Welsh gave him: Gerallt Gymro, Gerald the Welshman.",
  "His uncle David FitzGerald was Bishop of St Davids, and the boy was marked out for the Church. The story goes that while his brothers built castles of sand, Gerald built churches and monasteries. He learned his letters at St Davids and at Gloucester, and then went to Paris, the greatest school in Christendom, where he studied and taught for years and won a name as a brilliant speaker.",
  "## Archdeacon of Brecon",
  "Home again in 1175, he was made Archdeacon of Brecon, with his house at Llanddew beside the Usk. He threw himself into the work of the Church in Wales, and in 1176, when his uncle the bishop died, the canons of St Davids chose Gerald to follow him. King Henry II would not have it. He did not want a bishop at St Davids who was a kinsman of the Lord Rhys and who loved Wales so dearly, and the post went to another. It was the first time Gerald was kept from St Davids. It would not be the last.",
  "## At the king’s court",
  "In 1184 Henry II made him a royal clerk, and the next year sent him to Ireland with the young Prince John. Gerald came back with two books: The Topography of Ireland, full of the wonders, the birds and the beasts of that island, and The Conquest of Ireland, the story of the knights of his own family, the sons and grandsons of Nest, who had crossed the sea to win lands there. He read his Topography aloud at Oxford over three days to great crowds, and fed them all at his own expense.",
  "## The journey of 1188",
  "In March 1188 Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury, set out to ride round Wales and call its men to the Third Crusade, and Gerald, who knew the country and its people and spoke for them, went with him. At Radnor they were met by the Lord Rhys, prince of South Wales, and Gerald was the first man there to take the cross.[[cite:gerald-itin]]",
  "For seven weeks they travelled: through Hay and Brecon, Abergavenny, Usk and Caerleon, Cardiff and Llandaf, Margam and Swansea, Kidwelly and Carmarthen, to Haverfordwest and St Davids. They came north by Cardigan, where the Lord Rhys feasted them, by Strata Florida and Llanbadarn Fawr, across the Dyfi into Gwynedd, by Tywyn, Nefyn and Caernarfon to Bangor and Môn, and then east by Rhuddlan, St Asaph and Basingwerk, out of Wales at Chester. The princes of Gwynedd and Powys came to meet them on the way, and some three thousand men took the cross, among them many fine archers and spearmen.",
  "> Manorbier is the sweetest spot in Wales. | Gerald of Wales, The Journey through Wales, translated by Sir Richard Colt Hoare",
  "## The Journey through Wales",
  "Gerald’s account of the ride, Itinerarium Kambriae, came out in 1191. It is a travel book, a history and a treasure chest of stories all at once. He tells of every castle and church on the road, and of the saints and princes of each place. He tells how, in the reign of Henry I, three lords rode past the great lake of Brycheiniog, and one of them, Gruffudd ap Rhys, said that if he was the true prince of Wales the birds of the lake would declare it. At once the birds, beating the water with their wings, began to cry aloud, and proclaim him.[[cite:gerald-itin]]",
  "He tells of the beavers of the Teifi, the last in Britain, of the wonders of Môn, the corn store of Wales, of the stone of Llech Lafar at St Davids, and of the boy Elidyr, who visited the little people under the ground and learned their language. It is the first book ever written about a journey through Wales, and you can still follow his road today.",
  "## The Description of Wales",
  "In 1194 he wrote Descriptio Kambriae, The Description of Wales, a portrait of the Welsh people. Much of it is warm with pride. The Welsh, he says, are bold and quick-witted, sharp in argument and fearless in speech: nature has given confidence in speaking not only to the highest but to the humblest of them, even in the presence of their princes.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]]",
  "> No one of this nation ever begs, for the houses of all are common to all. | Gerald of Wales, The Description of Wales, book I, chapter 10",
  "He describes their hospitality: a traveller has only to walk in and hand over his weapons, and those who arrive in the morning are entertained till evening with the conversation of young women and the music of the harp.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]] He describes their music, how they do not sing in unison like the people of other countries, but in many parts, so that in a company of singers you hear as many parts as there are voices, all coming together in harmony.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]] He tells how they value noble birth and ancient descent above all things, so that even the common people know their family trees by heart, and how they love the poets, the harp and the old learning of the bards.",
  "## The answer of the old man of Pencader",
  "The Description ends with the most famous words ever written about Wales. When King Henry II marched against the Lord Rhys, he asked an old man at Pencader, who had joined his army, what he thought of the war, and whether the Welsh could hold out against him. Gerald gives his answer:",
  "> This nation, O king, may now, as in former times, be harassed, and in a great measure weakened and destroyed by your and other powers. But it will never be totally subdued by the wrath of man, unless the wrath of God shall concur. Nor do I think, that any other nation than this of Wales, or any other language, whatever may hereafter come to pass, shall, in the day of severe examination before the Supreme Judge, answer for this corner of the earth. | Gerald of Wales, The Description of Wales, book II, chapter 10",
  "Eight centuries later, the words of the old man of Pencader are cut in stone, read at Eisteddfodau and quoted wherever the Welsh speak of their country and their language. Gerald’s own lesson, written in the same chapter, is as famous: if the Welsh would be inseparable, they would become insuperable.[[cite:gerald-desc-2]]",
  "## The fight for St Davids",
  "When the Bishop of St Davids died in 1198, the canons chose Gerald once again. This time he set out to win not only the bishopric but the old dignity of St Davids itself. Welsh tradition held that Dewi had been an archbishop, and that St Davids had once been the head of the Church in Wales, answering to Rome alone and not to Canterbury. Gerald made that cause his own.",
  "Three times between 1199 and 1203 he went to Rome to plead before Pope Innocent III, crossing the Alps in winter and spending everything he had. The princes of Wales stood behind him. Llywelyn ap Iorwerth of Gwynedd and the other princes wrote to the Pope on his behalf, and he became the champion of the Welsh Church against the most powerful men in England. The Archbishop of Canterbury and King John opposed him at every turn, and in 1203 the Pope set both elections aside. Gerald had lost, but he had made the case for an independent Church in Wales that men would make again until it was won in 1920.",
  "## His last years",
  "Gerald gave up his archdeaconry to his nephew and spent his last twenty years writing: a book of instruction for princes, a mirror of the Church, the lives of saints, among them a Life of Saint David, and the story of his own life and of his battle for St Davids. He died about 1223. A stone figure of a churchman in St Davids Cathedral has long been called his, and his statue stands in the cathedral today, holding his book.",
  "## Why he matters",
  "No other writer of the Middle Ages tells us so much about Wales and the Welsh: how they lived, ate, fought, sang and argued, how they welcomed strangers and how they loved their land and their language. Without Gerald, the Wales of the princes would be a list of names and battles. With him, it is full of people. His books are still in print, still read in schools and still followed by walkers, and the old man of Pencader still speaks for Wales."],
 ["Daeth Gerallt Gymro â Chymru’r ddeuddegfed ganrif yn fyw fel na wnaeth unrhyw awdur arall. Yn 1188 marchogodd o amgylch y wlad gyfan, ac mae’r ddau lyfr a ysgrifennodd amdani, Hanes y Daith drwy Gymru a Disgrifiad o Gymru, yn dal i gael eu darllen a’u caru fwy nag wyth can mlynedd yn ddiweddarach. Treuliodd flynyddoedd gorau ei oes yn ymladd i wneud Tyddewi yn sedd archesgob i Gymru, yn rhydd o Gaergaint. Roedd yn falch o waed tywysogion Cymru yn ei wythiennau, ac mae Cymru wedi bod yn falch ohono byth ers hynny.",
  "|| Ganed | Tua 1146, yng Nghastell Maenorbŷr, Sir Benfro",
  "|| Ei dad | William de Barri, marchog Maenorbŷr",
  "|| Ei fam | Angharad, merch Nest ferch Rhys o Ddeheubarth",
  "|| Ei linach | Ŵyr i Nest, gorŵyr i Rys ap Tewdwr, Brenin Deheubarth, a châr i’r Arglwydd Rhys",
  "|| Swyddi | Archddiacon Brycheiniog o 1175; clerc brenhinol i’r Brenin Harri II o 1184",
  "|| Llyfrau | Tuag ugain, yn eu plith Hanes y Daith drwy Gymru (1191) a Disgrifiad o Gymru (1194)",
  "|| Bu farw | Tua 1223",
  "## Bachgen o Faenorbŷr",
  "Ganed Gerallt yng nghastell Maenorbŷr, ar arfordir Sir Benfro, ac nid anghofiodd mohono byth. Dyfed, meddai, oedd y rhan decaf o Gymru, Penfro y rhan decaf o Ddyfed, a Maenorbŷr y rhan decaf o Benfro, fel mai Maenorbŷr oedd y llecyn hyfrytaf yng Nghymru.[[cite:gerald-itin]] Marchog Normanaidd oedd ei dad, William de Barri. Ei fam, Angharad, oedd merch Nest, tywysoges enwocaf Cymru, a thrwyddi hi disgynnai Gerallt o Rys ap Tewdwr, brenin olaf Deheubarth cyn i’r Normaniaid ddod. Galwai ei hun wrth yr enw a roddodd y Cymry iddo: Gerallt Gymro.",
  "Roedd ei ewythr David FitzGerald yn Esgob Tyddewi, a nodwyd y bachgen i’r Eglwys. Yn ôl yr hanes, tra codai ei frodyr gestyll o dywod, codai Gerallt eglwysi a mynachlogydd. Dysgodd ei lythrennau yn Nhyddewi a Chaerloyw, ac yna aeth i Baris, ysgol fwyaf y byd Cristnogol, lle bu’n astudio ac yn dysgu am flynyddoedd ac ennill enw fel siaradwr disglair.",
  "## Archddiacon Brycheiniog",
  "Wedi dod adref yn 1175, fe’i gwnaed yn Archddiacon Brycheiniog, a’i dŷ yn Llan-ddew ger afon Wysg. Rhoddodd ei holl egni i waith yr Eglwys yng Nghymru, ac yn 1176, pan fu farw ei ewythr yr esgob, dewisodd canoniaid Tyddewi Gerallt i’w olynu. Ni fynnai’r Brenin Harri II hynny. Nid oedd am gael esgob yn Nhyddewi a oedd yn gâr i’r Arglwydd Rhys ac yn caru Cymru mor fawr, ac aeth y swydd i un arall. Dyna’r tro cyntaf y cadwyd Gerallt o Dyddewi. Nid hwnnw fyddai’r olaf.",
  "## Yn llys y brenin",
  "Yn 1184 gwnaeth Harri II ef yn glerc brenhinol, a’r flwyddyn wedyn anfonodd ef i Iwerddon gyda’r Tywysog John ifanc. Daeth Gerallt yn ôl â dau lyfr: Topograffeg Iwerddon, yn llawn o ryfeddodau, adar a bwystfilod yr ynys honno, a Goresgyniad Iwerddon, hanes marchogion ei deulu ei hun, meibion ac wyrion Nest, a groesodd y môr i ennill tiroedd yno. Darllenodd ei Dopograffeg yn uchel yn Rhydychen dros dridiau i dyrfaoedd mawr, a’u bwydo i gyd ar ei draul ei hun.",
  "## Taith 1188",
  "Ym mis Mawrth 1188 cychwynnodd Baldwin, Archesgob Caergaint, ar daith o amgylch Cymru i alw ei gwŷr i’r Drydedd Groesgad, ac aeth Gerallt, a adwaenai’r wlad a’i phobl ac a siaradai drostynt, gydag ef. Yn Llanandras daeth yr Arglwydd Rhys, tywysog y De, i’w cyfarfod, a Gerallt oedd y cyntaf yno i gymryd y groes.[[cite:gerald-itin]]",
  "Am saith wythnos teithiasant: drwy’r Gelli a Brycheiniog, y Fenni, Brynbuga a Chaerllion, Caerdydd a Llandaf, Margam ac Abertawe, Cydweli a Chaerfyrddin, i Hwlffordd a Thyddewi. Daethant i’r gogledd drwy Aberteifi, lle bu’r Arglwydd Rhys yn eu gwledda, drwy Ystrad Fflur a Llanbadarn Fawr, dros y Ddyfi i Wynedd, drwy Dywyn, Nefyn a Chaernarfon i Fangor a Môn, ac yna tua’r dwyrain drwy Ruddlan, Llanelwy a Maes Glas, gan adael Cymru yng Nghaer. Daeth tywysogion Gwynedd a Phowys i’w cyfarfod ar y ffordd, a chymerodd tua thair mil o wŷr y groes, yn eu plith lawer o saethwyr a gwaywffonwyr gwych.",
  "> Maenorbŷr yw’r llecyn hyfrytaf yng Nghymru. | Gerallt Gymro, Hanes y Daith drwy Gymru",
  "## Hanes y Daith drwy Gymru",
  "Daeth hanes Gerallt am y daith, Itinerarium Kambriae, allan yn 1191. Mae’n llyfr taith, yn hanes ac yn gist drysor o straeon ar yr un pryd. Mae’n sôn am bob castell ac eglwys ar y ffordd, ac am saint a thywysogion pob lle. Mae’n adrodd sut, yn nheyrnasiad Harri I, y marchogodd tri arglwydd heibio i lyn mawr Brycheiniog, a dywedodd un ohonynt, Gruffudd ap Rhys, mai os ef oedd gwir dywysog Cymru, y byddai adar y llyn yn cyhoeddi hynny. Ar unwaith dechreuodd yr adar guro’r dŵr â’u hadenydd a gweiddi’n uchel, a’i gyhoeddi ef.[[cite:gerald-itin]]",
  "Mae’n sôn am afancod Teifi, yr olaf ym Mhrydain, am ryfeddodau Môn, Môn Mam Cymru, am garreg Llech Lafar yn Nhyddewi, ac am y bachgen Elidyr, a ymwelodd â’r tylwyth teg dan y ddaear a dysgu eu hiaith. Dyma’r llyfr cyntaf erioed am daith drwy Gymru, a gallwch ddilyn ei ffordd hyd heddiw.",
  "## Disgrifiad o Gymru",
  "Yn 1194 ysgrifennodd Descriptio Kambriae, Disgrifiad o Gymru, darlun o’r Cymry. Mae llawer ohono’n gynnes gan falchder. Mae’r Cymry, meddai, yn eofn ac yn ffraeth, yn finiog mewn dadl ac yn ddi-ofn wrth siarad: rhoddodd natur hyder i siarad nid yn unig i’r uchaf ond i’r isaf ohonynt, hyd yn oed gerbron eu tywysogion.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]]",
  "> Nid oes neb o’r genedl hon byth yn cardota, oherwydd mae tai pawb yn agored i bawb. | Gerallt Gymro, Disgrifiad o Gymru, llyfr I, pennod 10",
  "Mae’n disgrifio eu lletygarwch: nid oes ond rhaid i deithiwr gerdded i mewn a rhoi ei arfau i’w cadw, a chaiff y rhai a ddaw yn y bore eu diddanu tan yr hwyr gan sgwrs merched ifanc a cherddoriaeth y delyn.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]] Mae’n disgrifio eu cerddoriaeth, sut nad ydynt yn canu’n unsain fel pobl gwledydd eraill, ond mewn llawer llais, fel bod cynifer o leisiau ag sydd o gantorion mewn cwmni, a’r cwbl yn dod ynghyd mewn cytgord.[[cite:gerald-desc-1]] Mae’n adrodd sut y prisient dras uchel ac achau hen uwchlaw popeth, fel bod hyd yn oed y werin yn gwybod eu hachau ar eu cof, a sut y carent y beirdd, y delyn a hen ddysg y beirdd.",
  "## Ateb hen ŵr Pencader",
  "Mae’r Disgrifiad yn gorffen â’r geiriau enwocaf a ysgrifennwyd erioed am Gymru. Pan orymdeithiodd y Brenin Harri II yn erbyn yr Arglwydd Rhys, gofynnodd i hen ŵr ym Mhencader, a oedd wedi ymuno â’i fyddin, beth a feddyliai am y rhyfel, ac a allai’r Cymry ddal eu tir yn ei erbyn. Dyma ei ateb yn ôl Gerallt:",
  "> Gellir yn awr, O frenin, fel yn yr amseroedd gynt, flino’r genedl hon, a’i gwanhau a’i difa i raddau mawr gan dy nerth di a nerthoedd eraill. Ond ni chaiff byth ei llwyr orchfygu gan lid dyn, oni bydd llid Duw yn cytuno. Ac ni thybiaf y bydd unrhyw genedl arall na hon o Gymru, nac unrhyw iaith arall, beth bynnag a ddigwydd wedi hyn, yn ateb dros y gornel fechan hon o’r ddaear ar ddydd y farn fawr gerbron y Barnwr Goruchaf. | Gerallt Gymro, Disgrifiad o Gymru, llyfr II, pennod 10",
  "Wyth canrif yn ddiweddarach, mae geiriau hen ŵr Pencader wedi’u naddu mewn carreg, yn cael eu darllen mewn Eisteddfodau ac yn cael eu dyfynnu lle bynnag y sonia’r Cymry am eu gwlad a’u hiaith. Mae gwers Gerallt ei hun, a ysgrifennwyd yn yr un bennod, yr un mor enwog: pe bai’r Cymry’n anwahanadwy, byddent yn anorchfygol.[[cite:gerald-desc-2]]",
  "## Y frwydr dros Dyddewi",
  "Pan fu farw Esgob Tyddewi yn 1198, dewisodd y canoniaid Gerallt unwaith eto. Y tro hwn aeth ati i ennill nid yn unig yr esgobaeth ond hen urddas Tyddewi ei hun. Daliai traddodiad Cymru fod Dewi wedi bod yn archesgob, a bod Tyddewi unwaith yn ben ar yr Eglwys yng Nghymru, yn atebol i Rufain yn unig ac nid i Gaergaint. Gwnaeth Gerallt yr achos hwnnw yn achos iddo ef ei hun.",
  "Deirgwaith rhwng 1199 a 1203 aeth i Rufain i ddadlau gerbron y Pab Innocent III, gan groesi’r Alpau yn y gaeaf a gwario popeth oedd ganddo. Safodd tywysogion Cymru y tu ôl iddo. Ysgrifennodd Llywelyn ab Iorwerth o Wynedd a’r tywysogion eraill at y Pab ar ei ran, a daeth yn bencampwr Eglwys Cymru yn erbyn gwŷr mwyaf grymus Lloegr. Gwrthwynebodd Archesgob Caergaint a’r Brenin John ef bob cam, ac yn 1203 rhoddodd y Pab y ddau etholiad o’r neilltu. Collasai Gerallt, ond roedd wedi gwneud yr achos dros Eglwys annibynnol yng Nghymru, achos y byddai eraill yn ei wneud eto nes ei ennill yn 1920.",
  "## Ei flynyddoedd olaf",
  "Rhoddodd Gerallt ei archddiaconiaeth i’w nai a threuliodd ei ugain mlynedd olaf yn ysgrifennu: llyfr o gyfarwyddyd i dywysogion, drych o’r Eglwys, bucheddau saint, yn eu plith Fuchedd Dewi, a hanes ei fywyd ei hun a’i frwydr dros Dyddewi. Bu farw tua 1223. Ers tro byd gelwir delw garreg o glerigwr yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Tyddewi yn ddelw iddo ef, ac mae ei gerflun yn sefyll yn yr eglwys gadeiriol heddiw, yn dal ei lyfr.",
  "## Pam mae’n bwysig",
  "Ni ddywed unrhyw awdur arall o’r Oesoedd Canol gymaint wrthym am Gymru a’r Cymry: sut roeddent yn byw, yn bwyta, yn ymladd, yn canu ac yn dadlau, sut y croesawent ddieithriaid a sut y carent eu tir a’u hiaith. Heb Gerallt, rhestr o enwau a brwydrau fyddai Cymru’r tywysogion. Gydag ef, mae’n llawn pobl. Mae ei lyfrau’n dal mewn print, yn dal i gael eu darllen mewn ysgolion a’u dilyn gan gerddwyr, ac mae hen ŵr Pencader yn dal i siarad dros Gymru."])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Nennius and the History of the Britons

LONG["nennius"] = (
 ["Around 829, in the reign of Merfyn Frych, King of Gwynedd, a scholar in Gwynedd gathered together everything he could find about the past of the Britons and wrote it down in Latin. His book, the Historia Brittonum, the History of the Britons, is the oldest history of Britain written in Wales, and it holds the earliest account of Arthur and his twelve battles. Later copies name its author as Nennius, a pupil of Elfoddw, the great churchman of Gwynedd.",
  "|| Book | Historia Brittonum, the History of the Britons",
  "|| Written | About 829–830, ‘in the fourth year of King Merfyn’",
  "|| Where | Gwynedd, at the court of Merfyn Frych",
  "|| His teacher | Elfoddw, archbishop of Gwynedd (died 809)",
  "|| Oldest copy | British Library, Harley MS 3859, written about 1100",
  "## The heap of all that he found",
  "The writer tells us how he worked. He had gathered what he could from the annals of the Romans, the chronicles of the holy fathers, the writings of the Irish and the Saxons, and the traditions of the elders of his own people. ‘I have made a heap of all that I found,’ he wrote, as if apologising for his plain style, and he asked his readers to forgive him. That heap is a treasure. Much of what it keeps is found nowhere else.",
  "Some copies end the reckoning of the years at the fourth year of King Merfyn, and one names its author as a disciple of Saint Elfoddw.[[cite:hb-giles]] That places the book at the court of Gwynedd around 829 or 830, when Merfyn Frych, founder of the line of Rhodri Mawr, welcomed scholars from Ireland and across the sea.",
  "## What it tells",
  "The book begins with the first peoples of the island and with Brutus, the Trojan prince from whom Britain took its name. It tells of the Romans, of Macsen Wledig and the British soldiers who settled in Armorica, of Saint Germanus, and of Gwrtheyrn, Vortigern, who invited Hengist and the Saxons into the island. It tells of the boy Emrys and the two dragons, the red and the white, who fought beneath the hill of Dinas Emrys in Eryri, and of how the red dragon drove the white away: the oldest telling of the dragon of Wales.",
  "It tells of Cunedda, who came south from Manaw Gododdin with his sons and drove the Irish out of Gwynedd. It tells of Urien Rheged and the kings of the North who besieged the Angles on Lindisfarne. And it names the first poets of the Welsh language: Talhaearn Tad Awen, Aneirin, Taliesin, Blwchfardd and Cian, who sang in the British tongue in the days of Ida of Bernicia.[[cite:hb-giles]]",
  "## Arthur and the twelve battles",
  "Best known of all is its chapter on Arthur. ‘Then it was,’ it says, ‘that the magnanimous Arthur, with all the kings and military force of Britain, fought against the Saxons.’[[cite:hb-giles]] It names his twelve battles: at the mouth of the river Glein, four on the river Dubglas, on the Bassas, in the wood of Celyddon, at Castellum Guinnion, where he carried the image of the Virgin Mary on his shoulders, at the City of the Legion, on the Tribruit, on the hill called Agned, and the twelfth on the hill of Badon, where the Britons won the day.",
  "Welsh tradition holds that this Arthur was Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing, whose grants of land are written in the Book of Llandaf, and that the City of the Legion was Caerleon on Usk. Other historians may see it differently. The book also counts Arthur’s marvels among the wonders of Britain: the stone at Carn Cabal in Buellt that bears the footprint of his hound, and the grave of his son Amr at the source of the Gamber, which is never the same length twice.",
  "## The book that travelled",
  "The History of the Britons was copied all over Europe: some forty copies survive, in libraries from London to Rome, and it was turned into Irish as Lebor Bretnach. The oldest copy, made about 1100, keeps alongside it the Annales Cambriae, the Welsh annals, and the family trees of the early kings of Wales and the Old North. Three hundred years after it was written, Geoffrey of Monmouth built his great History of the Kings of Britain on its foundations, and through Geoffrey the story of Arthur went round the world."],
 ["Tua 829, yn nheyrnasiad Merfyn Frych, Brenin Gwynedd, casglodd ysgolhaig yng Ngwynedd y cyfan a allai ei gael am orffennol y Brythoniaid a’i ysgrifennu yn Lladin. Ei lyfr, yr Historia Brittonum, Hanes y Brythoniaid, yw’r hanes hynaf o Brydain a ysgrifennwyd yng Nghymru, ac ynddo mae’r hanes cynharaf am Arthur a’i ddeuddeg brwydr. Mae copïau diweddarach yn enwi’r awdur yn Nennius, disgybl i Elfoddw, eglwyswr mawr Gwynedd.",
  "|| Llyfr | Historia Brittonum, Hanes y Brythoniaid",
  "|| Ysgrifennwyd | Tua 829–830, ‘ym mhedwaredd flwyddyn y Brenin Merfyn’",
  "|| Ble | Gwynedd, yn llys Merfyn Frych",
  "|| Ei athro | Elfoddw, archesgob Gwynedd (bu farw 809)",
  "|| Y copi hynaf | Y Llyfrgell Brydeinig, Harley MS 3859, a ysgrifennwyd tua 1100",
  "## Pentwr o’r cyfan a gafodd",
  "Mae’r awdur yn dweud wrthym sut y gweithiai. Casglodd yr hyn a allai o flwyddnodion y Rhufeiniaid, croniclau’r tadau sanctaidd, ysgrifeniadau’r Gwyddyl a’r Saeson, a thraddodiadau henuriaid ei bobl ei hun. ‘Gwneuthum bentwr o’r cyfan a gefais,’ meddai, fel petai’n ymddiheuro am ei arddull blaen, a gofynnodd i’w ddarllenwyr faddau iddo. Trysor yw’r pentwr hwnnw. Ni cheir llawer o’r hyn a geidw yn unman arall.",
  "Mae rhai copïau’n gorffen cyfrif y blynyddoedd ym mhedwaredd flwyddyn y Brenin Merfyn, ac mae un yn enwi’r awdur yn ddisgybl i Sant Elfoddw.[[cite:hb-giles]] Mae hynny’n gosod y llyfr yn llys Gwynedd tua 829 neu 830, pan groesawai Merfyn Frych, sylfaenydd llinach Rhodri Mawr, ysgolheigion o Iwerddon a thros y môr.",
  "## Yr hyn y mae’n ei adrodd",
  "Mae’r llyfr yn dechrau â phobloedd cyntaf yr ynys ac â Brutus, y tywysog o Gaerdroea y cafodd Prydain ei henw ganddo. Mae’n sôn am y Rhufeiniaid, am Facsen Wledig a’r milwyr Brythonig a ymsefydlodd yn Llydaw, am Sant Garmon, ac am Wrtheyrn, a wahoddodd Hengist a’r Saeson i’r ynys. Mae’n adrodd am y bachgen Emrys a’r ddwy ddraig, y goch a’r wen, a ymladdai dan fryn Dinas Emrys yn Eryri, a sut y gyrrodd y ddraig goch y wen ymaith: yr hanes hynaf am ddraig Cymru.",
  "Mae’n adrodd am Gunedda, a ddaeth i’r de o Fanaw Gododdin gyda’i feibion a gyrru’r Gwyddyl o Wynedd. Mae’n sôn am Urien Rheged a brenhinoedd y Gogledd a fu’n gwarchae ar yr Eingl ar Ynys Metcaud. Ac mae’n enwi beirdd cyntaf yr iaith Gymraeg: Talhaearn Tad Awen, Aneirin, Taliesin, Blwchfardd a Chian, a ganai yn yr iaith Frythoneg yn nyddiau Ida o Bernicia.[[cite:hb-giles]]",
  "## Arthur a’r deuddeg brwydr",
  "Yr enwocaf oll yw ei bennod ar Arthur. ‘Yna,’ meddai, ‘yr ymladdodd Arthur fawrfrydig, gyda holl frenhinoedd a llu Prydain, yn erbyn y Saeson.’[[cite:hb-giles]] Mae’n enwi ei ddeuddeg brwydr: wrth aber afon Glein, pedair ar afon Dubglas, ar y Bassas, yng Nghoed Celyddon, yng Nghastell Guinnion, lle cariodd ddelw’r Forwyn Fair ar ei ysgwyddau, yng Nghaer y Lleng, ar y Tryfrwyd, ar y bryn a elwir Agned, a’r ddeuddegfed ar Fynydd Baddon, lle cariodd y Brythoniaid y dydd.",
  "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig, brenin Gwent a Glywysing, oedd yr Arthur hwn, y mae ei roddion tir wedi’u hysgrifennu yn Llyfr Llandaf, a Chaerllion ar Wysg oedd Caer y Lleng. Gall haneswyr eraill weld pethau’n wahanol. Mae’r llyfr hefyd yn cyfrif rhyfeddodau Arthur ymhlith rhyfeddodau Prydain: y garreg ar Garn Gabal ym Muellt sy’n dwyn ôl troed ei gi, a bedd ei fab Amr wrth darddiad afon Gamber, nad yw byth yr un hyd ddwywaith.",
  "## Y llyfr a deithiodd",
  "Copïwyd Hanes y Brythoniaid ledled Ewrop: mae tua deugain copi wedi goroesi, mewn llyfrgelloedd o Lundain i Rufain, a throwyd ef i’r Wyddeleg fel Lebor Bretnach. Mae’r copi hynaf, a wnaed tua 1100, yn cadw wrth ei ochr yr Annales Cambriae, cronicl Cymru, ac achau brenhinoedd cynnar Cymru a’r Hen Ogledd. Dri chan mlynedd ar ôl ei ysgrifennu, adeiladodd Sieffre o Fynwy ei Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain mawr ar ei sylfeini, a thrwy Sieffre aeth hanes Arthur o amgylch y byd."])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Asser

LONG["asser"] = (
 ["Asser was a monk and scholar of St Davids who became the friend and teacher of Alfred the Great, King of Wessex, and wrote the first life ever written of an English king. A Welshman wrote the story of England’s most famous king, and the learning of St Davids helped to revive learning in England.",
  "|| Home | The monastery of St Davids, Mynyw, in Dyfed",
  "|| Kinsman | Nobis, Bishop of St Davids",
  "|| Called to Wessex | About 885, by King Alfred",
  "|| Book | The Life of King Alfred, written in 893",
  "|| Later | Bishop of Sherborne",
  "|| Died | 908 or 909",
  "## The call from Alfred",
  "Asser grew up and was trained at St Davids, where his kinsman Nobis was bishop. His learning was known far beyond Wales, and around 885 King Alfred sent for him. Alfred wanted to bring back the learning that the long wars with the Danes had destroyed in England, and he gathered round him the best scholars he could find.",
  "Asser tells the story himself. They met at Dean in Sussex, and the king asked him to leave everything in Wales and join his household. Asser would not leave St Davids without the consent of his brothers there, and in the end he agreed to spend half of every year with the king and half at home. The monks of St Davids gave their blessing, Asser writes, because they hoped that the friendship of so great a king would protect their church.",
  "## Teacher to a king",
  "For years he read with Alfred, day and night, and helped him with his own great work of turning the best books of the age into English. Asser tells how one day the king, listening to a passage he liked, asked Asser to write it into the little book he carried everywhere, and how from that day Alfred began to read and translate Latin for himself.",
  "## The Life of King Alfred",
  "In 893 Asser wrote the Life of King Alfred, the first biography of an English king. It tells of Alfred’s childhood, of his long struggle against the Danes, of his laws, his buildings and his love of books, and it is still the main source for his life.",
  "Asser wrote as a Welshman. He gives the British names of English places: Nottingham, he says, is called in the British tongue Tig Guocobauc, the house of caves. And he tells how the kings of Wales came to Alfred: Hyfaidd of Dyfed, Elisedd of Brycheiniog, Hywel ap Rhys of Glywysing, Brochfael and Ffernfael of Gwent, and at last Anarawd, son of Rhodri Mawr, whom Alfred received with honour and great gifts.[[cite:asser-giles]]",
  "## Bishop of Sherborne",
  "Alfred rewarded him with the monasteries of Congresbury and Banwell, then Exeter, and in the end made him Bishop of Sherborne. He died in 908 or 909, a son of St Davids who had served one of the great kings of Europe and written his story. Every life of Alfred written since begins with Asser."],
 ["Mynach ac ysgolhaig o Dyddewi oedd Asser a ddaeth yn gyfaill ac yn athro i Alffred Fawr, Brenin Wessex, ac a ysgrifennodd y cofiant cyntaf erioed i frenin o Sais. Cymro a ysgrifennodd hanes brenin enwocaf Lloegr, a helpodd dysg Tyddewi i adfywio dysg yn Lloegr.",
  "|| Cartref | Mynachlog Tyddewi, Mynyw, yn Nyfed",
  "|| Câr | Nobis, Esgob Tyddewi",
  "|| Galwyd i Wessex | Tua 885, gan y Brenin Alffred",
  "|| Llyfr | Buchedd y Brenin Alffred, a ysgrifennwyd yn 893",
  "|| Wedyn | Esgob Sherborne",
  "|| Bu farw | 908 neu 909",
  "## Galwad Alffred",
  "Magwyd a hyfforddwyd Asser yn Nhyddewi, lle’r oedd ei gâr Nobis yn esgob. Roedd ei ddysg yn hysbys ymhell y tu hwnt i Gymru, a thua 885 anfonodd y Brenin Alffred amdano. Roedd Alffred am adfer y ddysg a ddinistriwyd yn Lloegr gan y rhyfeloedd hir â’r Daniaid, a chasglodd o’i gwmpas yr ysgolheigion gorau y gallai eu cael.",
  "Mae Asser yn adrodd yr hanes ei hun. Cyfarfuant yn Dean yn Sussex, a gofynnodd y brenin iddo adael popeth yng Nghymru ac ymuno â’i lys. Ni fynnai Asser adael Tyddewi heb ganiatâd ei frodyr yno, ac yn y diwedd cytunodd i dreulio hanner pob blwyddyn gyda’r brenin a hanner gartref. Rhoddodd mynachod Tyddewi eu bendith, medd Asser, am eu bod yn gobeithio y byddai cyfeillgarwch brenin mor fawr yn amddiffyn eu heglwys.",
  "## Athro i frenin",
  "Am flynyddoedd bu’n darllen gydag Alffred, ddydd a nos, a’i helpu gyda’i waith mawr ei hun o droi llyfrau gorau’r oes i’r Saesneg. Mae Asser yn adrodd sut, un diwrnod, y gofynnodd y brenin, wrth wrando ar ddarn a hoffai, i Asser ei ysgrifennu yn y llyfr bach a gariai i bobman, a sut o’r diwrnod hwnnw y dechreuodd Alffred ddarllen a chyfieithu Lladin ei hun.",
  "## Buchedd y Brenin Alffred",
  "Yn 893 ysgrifennodd Asser Fuchedd y Brenin Alffred, y cofiant cyntaf i frenin o Sais. Mae’n adrodd am blentyndod Alffred, am ei frwydr hir yn erbyn y Daniaid, am ei gyfreithiau, ei adeiladau a’i gariad at lyfrau, a dyma’r brif ffynhonnell am ei fywyd o hyd.",
  "Ysgrifennai Asser fel Cymro. Mae’n rhoi enwau Brythoneg lleoedd yn Lloegr: gelwir Nottingham, meddai, yn yr iaith Frythoneg yn Tig Guocobauc, tŷ’r ogofâu. Ac mae’n adrodd sut y daeth brenhinoedd Cymru at Alffred: Hyfaidd o Ddyfed, Elisedd o Frycheiniog, Hywel ap Rhys o Glywysing, Brochfael a Ffernfael o Went, ac yn y diwedd Anarawd, mab Rhodri Mawr, a dderbyniodd Alffred ag anrhydedd a rhoddion mawr.[[cite:asser-giles]]",
  "## Esgob Sherborne",
  "Gwobrwyodd Alffred ef â mynachlogydd Congresbury a Banwell, yna Caerwysg, ac yn y diwedd fe’i gwnaeth yn Esgob Sherborne. Bu farw yn 908 neu 909, mab i Dyddewi a fu’n gwasanaethu un o frenhinoedd mawr Ewrop ac a ysgrifennodd ei hanes. Mae pob buchedd i Alffred a ysgrifennwyd ers hynny yn dechrau gydag Asser."])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Geoffrey of Monmouth

LONG["geoffrey-of-monmouth"] = (
 ["Geoffrey of Monmouth, Sieffre o Fynwy, wrote the most famous book of the Middle Ages about Britain. His History of the Kings of Britain told the story of the island from Brutus of Troy to Cadwaladr, the last high king of the Britons, and carried Arthur, Merlin, King Lear and Cymbeline to every court in Europe. He was a man of Gwent, and he wrote the history of the Britons as the history of the whole island.",
  "|| Born | About 1095, probably at Monmouth, in Gwent",
  "|| Called himself | Galfridus Monemutensis, Geoffrey of Monmouth",
  "|| Work | Canon of St George’s, Oxford, from about 1129",
  "|| Books | The Prophecies of Merlin (about 1135), The History of the Kings of Britain (about 1136), The Life of Merlin (about 1150)",
  "|| Bishop | Of St Asaph, consecrated 1152",
  "|| Died | 1155",
  "## A man of Gwent",
  "Geoffrey always called himself ‘of Monmouth’, and his love for the south-east of Wales runs through his book. Caerleon on Usk is the most splendid city in it: there Arthur holds his great crowning feast, in a city of golden roofs beside the river, with its two churches and its school of two hundred learned men. He spent his working life in Oxford, as a canon of the college of St George in Oxford Castle, among scholars who knew and valued the old learning of the Britons.",
  "## The very ancient book",
  "Geoffrey said that he had not made his history up. Walter, Archdeacon of Oxford, had given him ‘a very ancient book in the British tongue’, telling the deeds of all the kings of Britain from Brutus to Cadwaladr, and Geoffrey had turned it into Latin.[[cite:geoffrey-evans]] Welsh tradition holds that this old book was a chronicle of the British kings kept in Wales, and later Welsh copies of the history, among them the version named after Tysilio, have been held to be that book or close to it.",
  "## The History of the Kings of Britain",
  "The History begins with Brutus, great-grandson of Aeneas of Troy, who led the Trojans to the island of Albion, named it Britain after himself, and founded New Troy on the Thames. His three sons divide the island: Locrinus has Lloegr, Camber has Cymru, and Albanactus has Alban, Scotland. Then come the kings: Bladud, who founded Bath; Leir and his three daughters; Dyfnwal Moelmud, the lawgiver; Beli and Brân, who took Rome itself; Lludd, who rebuilt London and gave it his name; Caswallon, who drove Julius Caesar back to the sea; Cymbeline, Arthfael, Lucius, the first Christian king; Constantine and Macsen.",
  "Then the story turns to the long fight with the Saxons: Vortigern and the dragons of Dinas Emrys, the boy Merlin and his prophecies, Ambrosius and Uther, and above all Arthur, who wins back the island, conquers lands across the sea, holds court at Caerleon and is carried at last, wounded, to the isle of Avalon. It ends with Cadwaladr, who is told by an angel’s voice that the Britons will one day win back their land.",
  "## The book that conquered Europe",
  "No book written in Britain in the Middle Ages was read more widely. More than two hundred copies in Latin survive, more than of any other history of its time. It was turned into French, English and Norse, and above all into Welsh, as Brut y Brenhinedd, the History of the Kings, of which some sixty copies were made in medieval Wales. From it came the romances of Arthur and the Round Table, Shakespeare’s King Lear and Cymbeline, Spenser’s Faerie Queene and the Tudor pride in their British descent.",
  "## Bishop of St Asaph",
  "In 1152 Geoffrey was consecrated Bishop of St Asaph, Llanelwy, in north-east Wales. He died in 1155. For the Welsh his book was far more than a story. It said that the Britons had been the first and rightful people of the whole island, the heirs of Troy and of Rome, and that their day would come again. Poets, princes and Owain Glyndŵr himself drew on it, and Henry Tudor marched to Bosworth under the red dragon of Cadwaladr, the last king of Geoffrey’s History."],
 ["Ysgrifennodd Sieffre o Fynwy y llyfr enwocaf o’r Oesoedd Canol am Brydain. Adroddai ei Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain stori’r ynys o Frutus o Gaerdroea hyd Gadwaladr, goruchaf frenin olaf y Brythoniaid, a chariodd Arthur, Myrddin, y Brenin Llŷr a Chynfelyn i bob llys yn Ewrop. Gŵr o Went ydoedd, ac ysgrifennodd hanes y Brythoniaid fel hanes yr ynys gyfan.",
  "|| Ganed | Tua 1095, ym Mynwy yn ôl pob tebyg, yng Ngwent",
  "|| Galwai ei hun | Galfridus Monemutensis, Sieffre o Fynwy",
  "|| Gwaith | Canon Sant Siôr, Rhydychen, o tua 1129",
  "|| Llyfrau | Proffwydoliaethau Myrddin (tua 1135), Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain (tua 1136), Buchedd Myrddin (tua 1150)",
  "|| Esgob | Llanelwy, a gysegrwyd yn 1152",
  "|| Bu farw | 1155",
  "## Gŵr o Went",
  "Galwai Sieffre ei hun yn wastad ‘o Fynwy’, ac mae ei gariad at dde-ddwyrain Cymru yn rhedeg drwy ei lyfr. Caerllion ar Wysg yw’r ddinas wychaf ynddo: yno mae Arthur yn cynnal gwledd fawr ei goroni, mewn dinas o doeau aur ar lan yr afon, gyda’i dwy eglwys a’i hysgol o ddau gant o wŷr dysgedig. Treuliodd ei oes waith yn Rhydychen, yn ganon coleg Sant Siôr yng Nghastell Rhydychen, ymhlith ysgolheigion a adwaenai ac a brisiai hen ddysg y Brythoniaid.",
  "## Yr hen lyfr",
  "Dywedodd Sieffre nad oedd wedi dyfeisio ei hanes. Roedd Walter, Archddiacon Rhydychen, wedi rhoi iddo ‘hen lyfr yn yr iaith Frythoneg’, yn adrodd gweithredoedd holl frenhinoedd Prydain o Frutus hyd Gadwaladr, ac roedd Sieffre wedi’i droi i’r Lladin.[[cite:geoffrey-evans]] Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, cronicl o frenhinoedd y Brythoniaid a gadwyd yng Nghymru oedd yr hen lyfr hwn, a chredwyd mai copïau Cymraeg diweddarach o’r hanes, yn eu plith y fersiwn a enwir ar ôl Tysilio, yw’r llyfr hwnnw neu’n agos ato.",
  "## Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain",
  "Mae’r Hanes yn dechrau gyda Brutus, gorŵyr Aeneas o Gaerdroea, a arweiniodd y Troeaid i ynys Albion, ei henwi’n Brydain ar ei ôl ei hun, a sefydlu Caerdroea Newydd ar lan Tafwys. Mae ei dri mab yn rhannu’r ynys: Locrinus yn cael Lloegr, Camber yn cael Cymru, ac Albanactus yn cael yr Alban. Yna daw’r brenhinoedd: Bleiddud, a sefydlodd Gaerfaddon; Llŷr a’i dair merch; Dyfnwal Moelmud, y deddfwr; Beli a Brân, a gipiodd Rufain ei hun; Lludd, a ailgododd Lundain a rhoi ei enw iddi; Caswallon, a yrrodd Iŵl Cesar yn ôl i’r môr; Cynfelyn, Arthfael, Lles, y brenin Cristnogol cyntaf; Cystennin a Macsen.",
  "Yna mae’r stori’n troi at yr ymdrech hir â’r Saeson: Gwrtheyrn a dreigiau Dinas Emrys, y bachgen Myrddin a’i broffwydoliaethau, Emrys ac Uthr, ac yn bennaf oll Arthur, sy’n ennill yr ynys yn ôl, yn gorchfygu tiroedd dros y môr, yn cynnal llys yng Nghaerllion ac yn cael ei gludo o’r diwedd, yn glwyfedig, i ynys Afallon. Mae’n gorffen gyda Chadwaladr, y dywed llais angel wrtho y bydd y Brythoniaid un dydd yn ennill eu tir yn ôl.",
  "## Y llyfr a orchfygodd Ewrop",
  "Ni ddarllenwyd unrhyw lyfr a ysgrifennwyd ym Mhrydain yn yr Oesoedd Canol yn ehangach. Mae mwy na dau gant o gopïau Lladin wedi goroesi, mwy nag o unrhyw hanes arall o’i gyfnod. Fe’i trowyd i’r Ffrangeg, y Saesneg a’r Norseg, ac yn bennaf oll i’r Gymraeg, fel Brut y Brenhinedd, y gwnaed tua thrigain copi ohono yng Nghymru’r Oesoedd Canol. Ohono y daeth rhamantau Arthur a’r Ford Gron, King Lear a Cymbeline Shakespeare, Faerie Queene Spenser a balchder y Tuduriaid yn eu tras Frythonig.",
  "## Esgob Llanelwy",
  "Yn 1152 cysegrwyd Sieffre yn Esgob Llanelwy, yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru. Bu farw yn 1155. I’r Cymry roedd ei lyfr yn llawer mwy na stori. Dywedai mai’r Brythoniaid oedd pobl gyntaf a chyfiawn yr ynys gyfan, etifeddion Caerdroea a Rhufain, ac y deuai eu dydd eto. Tynnodd beirdd, tywysogion ac Owain Glyndŵr ei hun arno, a gorymdeithiodd Harri Tudur i Fosworth dan ddraig goch Cadwaladr, brenin olaf Hanes Sieffre."])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Rhygyfarch

LONG["rhygyfarch"] = (
 ["Rhygyfarch ap Sulien was the finest scholar of eleventh-century Wales. At Llanbadarn Fawr, near Aberystwyth, he wrote the Life of Saint David, the book that made Dewi known across Christendom and gave Wales its patron saint. He was a poet too, and his lament for Wales under the first Norman attacks is one of the most moving poems of the age.",
  "|| Born | 1057, son of Sulien, later Bishop of St Davids",
  "|| Home | Llanbadarn Fawr, Ceredigion",
  "|| Brothers | Ieuan, Arthen and Daniel, all scholars",
  "|| Books | The Life of Saint David (about 1095), Latin poems, the Rhygyfarch Psalter",
  "|| Died | 1099",
  "## The family of Llanbadarn",
  "Rhygyfarch’s father, Sulien, had studied for years in Scotland and Ireland and came home to run a famous school at Llanbadarn Fawr. Twice he was Bishop of St Davids. His four sons all became scholars, and the family made Llanbadarn the brightest centre of learning in Wales: they wrote Latin poetry, copied and decorated books, and kept the old learning of the Britons alive at the moment the Normans arrived.",
  "## The Psalter",
  "Around 1080 the family made a book of the Psalms, now in Trinity College, Dublin, and known as the Rhygyfarch Psalter. It was written by the scribe Ithael and decorated by Rhygyfarch’s brother Ieuan, with great letters of knotwork, birds and beasts in red, green and gold. Rhygyfarch wrote verses in it himself. It is one of the most beautiful books ever made in Wales.",
  "## The Life of Saint David",
  "Around 1095 Rhygyfarch wrote Buchedd Dewi in Latin, from the old writings he had found, as he says, in the ancient books of St Davids. It tells of Dewi’s birth to Non on the cliffs in a storm, of his schooling under Paulinus, of the monasteries he founded, of his strict and gentle rule, of the synod at Brefi where the ground rose beneath his feet so that all could hear him, and of his last words to his monks.",
  "The Life was copied all over Britain and beyond. It is the reason Dewi became a saint for the whole Church, why his shrine at St Davids drew pilgrims from across Europe, and why the first of March is the day of Wales.",
  "## The lament for Wales",
  "When the Normans broke into Ceredigion in the 1090s, Rhygyfarch wrote a lament in Latin verse. He mourns that the people and the clergy are despised, that the law is broken and learning silenced, and he prays for his country. It is the first great poem of protest written in Wales.",
  "He died in 1099, only forty-two years old. The chronicle of the princes mourns him as the wisest of the learned men of the Britons, who had had no equal before him, and it is Rhygyfarch’s Dewi whom Wales still honours every first of March."],
 ["Rhygyfarch ap Sulien oedd ysgolhaig gorau Cymru’r unfed ganrif ar ddeg. Yn Llanbadarn Fawr, ger Aberystwyth, ysgrifennodd Fuchedd Dewi, y llyfr a wnaeth Dewi’n adnabyddus drwy’r byd Cristnogol a rhoi i Gymru ei nawddsant. Roedd yn fardd hefyd, ac mae ei alarnad dros Gymru dan yr ymosodiadau Normanaidd cyntaf yn un o gerddi mwyaf teimladwy’r oes.",
  "|| Ganed | 1057, mab Sulien, Esgob Tyddewi wedi hynny",
  "|| Cartref | Llanbadarn Fawr, Ceredigion",
  "|| Brodyr | Ieuan, Arthen a Daniel, pob un yn ysgolhaig",
  "|| Llyfrau | Buchedd Dewi (tua 1095), cerddi Lladin, Sallwyr Rhygyfarch",
  "|| Bu farw | 1099",
  "## Teulu Llanbadarn",
  "Roedd tad Rhygyfarch, Sulien, wedi astudio am flynyddoedd yn yr Alban ac Iwerddon a daeth adref i gadw ysgol enwog yn Llanbadarn Fawr. Ddwywaith bu’n Esgob Tyddewi. Daeth ei bedwar mab i gyd yn ysgolheigion, a gwnaeth y teulu Lanbadarn yn ganolfan ddysg ddisgleiriaf Cymru: ysgrifennent farddoniaeth Ladin, copïent ac addurnent lyfrau, a chadwasant hen ddysg y Brythoniaid yn fyw yr union adeg y cyrhaeddodd y Normaniaid.",
  "## Y Sallwyr",
  "Tua 1080 gwnaeth y teulu lyfr o’r Salmau, sydd heddiw yng Ngholeg y Drindod, Dulyn, ac a elwir yn Sallwyr Rhygyfarch. Fe’i hysgrifennwyd gan y copïydd Ithael a’i addurno gan Ieuan, brawd Rhygyfarch, â phriflythrennau mawr o gwlwm, adar a bwystfilod mewn coch, gwyrdd ac aur. Ysgrifennodd Rhygyfarch benillion ynddo ei hun. Mae’n un o’r llyfrau harddaf a wnaed erioed yng Nghymru.",
  "## Buchedd Dewi",
  "Tua 1095 ysgrifennodd Rhygyfarch Fuchedd Dewi yn Lladin, o’r hen ysgrifeniadau a gafodd, meddai, yn hen lyfrau Tyddewi. Mae’n adrodd am eni Dewi i Non ar y clogwyni mewn storm, am ei addysg dan Bawlinus, am y mynachlogydd a sefydlodd, am ei reol lem a thyner, am y synod yn Llanddewi Brefi lle cododd y tir dan ei draed fel y gallai pawb ei glywed, ac am ei eiriau olaf wrth ei fynachod.",
  "Copïwyd y Fuchedd ledled Prydain a thu hwnt. Dyna’r rheswm y daeth Dewi’n sant i’r Eglwys gyfan, pam y denai ei gysegrfa yn Nhyddewi bererinion o bob rhan o Ewrop, a pham mai’r cyntaf o Fawrth yw diwrnod Cymru.",
  "## Y galarnad dros Gymru",
  "Pan dorrodd y Normaniaid i Geredigion yn y 1090au, ysgrifennodd Rhygyfarch alarnad mewn mydr Lladin. Mae’n galaru bod y bobl a’r clerigwyr yn cael eu dirmygu, bod y gyfraith wedi’i thorri a dysg wedi’i thewi, ac mae’n gweddïo dros ei wlad. Dyma’r gerdd brotest fawr gyntaf a ysgrifennwyd yng Nghymru.",
  "Bu farw yn 1099, yn ddim ond dwy a deugain oed. Mae cronicl y tywysogion yn ei alaru fel y doethaf o wŷr dysgedig y Brythoniaid, na fu ei gyffelyb o’i flaen, a Dewi Rhygyfarch y mae Cymru’n dal i’w anrhydeddu bob cyntaf o Fawrth."])


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the new people
PEOPLE = [
 P("elfoddw", "kings", "d. 809", "Elfoddw", "Elfoddw", "Archbishop of Gwynedd", "Archesgob Gwynedd", "Elfodd", "faith",
   "The great churchman of Gwynedd who brought the Britons into line with Rome over the date of Easter in 768, and the teacher named in the History of the Britons.",
   "Eglwyswr mawr Gwynedd a ddaeth â’r Brythoniaid i gytundeb â Rhufain ar ddyddiad y Pasg yn 768, a’r athro a enwir yn Hanes y Brythoniaid.",
   ["Elfoddw was the leading churchman of north Wales at the end of the eighth century. The Annales Cambriae call him ‘archbishop in the land of Gwynedd’ when they record his death in 809, and Welsh tradition places him at Bangor.",
    "For two hundred years the Britons had kept their own way of reckoning Easter, older than the one used in Rome. In 768, the annals say, Easter was changed among the Britons, and it was Elfoddw, ‘servant of God’, who set it right.[[cite:ac-wales]] It was a great change, made peacefully, and it brought the Church of Wales into step with the rest of Christendom.",
    "He was remembered above all as a teacher. One copy of the Historia Brittonum names its author as a disciple of Saint Elfoddw, so that the first history of Britain written in Wales came out of his school.[[cite:hb-giles]]"],
   ["Elfoddw oedd prif eglwyswr gogledd Cymru ar ddiwedd yr wythfed ganrif. Mae’r Annales Cambriae yn ei alw’n ‘archesgob yng ngwlad Gwynedd’ wrth gofnodi ei farw yn 809, ac mae traddodiad Cymru yn ei osod ym Mangor.",
    "Am ddau gan mlynedd roedd y Brythoniaid wedi cadw eu ffordd eu hunain o gyfrif y Pasg, yn hŷn na’r un a ddefnyddid yn Rhufain. Yn 768, medd y cronicl, newidiwyd y Pasg ymhlith y Brythoniaid, ac Elfoddw, ‘gwas Duw’, a’i cywirodd.[[cite:ac-wales]] Roedd yn newid mawr, a wnaed yn heddychlon, a daeth ag Eglwys Cymru i gam â gweddill y byd Cristnogol.",
    "Fe’i cofiwyd yn bennaf fel athro. Mae un copi o’r Historia Brittonum yn enwi ei hawdur yn ddisgybl i Sant Elfoddw, felly o’i ysgol ef y daeth yr hanes cyntaf o Brydain a ysgrifennwyd yng Nghymru.[[cite:hb-giles]]"]),
 P("lifris", "kings", "fl. c. 1100", "Lifris of Llancarfan", "Lifris o Lancarfan", "Biographer of Saint Cadog", "Cofiannydd Sant Cadog", "", "learning",
   "Archdeacon of Glamorgan and master of the school of Llancarfan, who wrote the Life of Saint Cadog around 1100, one of the earliest books to tell of King Arthur.",
   "Archddiacon Morgannwg ac athro ysgol Llancarfan, a ysgrifennodd Fuchedd Sant Cadog tua 1100, un o’r llyfrau cynharaf i sôn am y Brenin Arthur.",
   ["Lifris was the son of Herewald, Bishop of Llandaf in the second half of the eleventh century. He became Archdeacon of Glamorgan and master of Llancarfan, the great monastery in the Vale of Glamorgan that Saint Cadog had founded five hundred years before.",
    "Around 1100 he wrote the Life of Saint Cadog in Latin. It tells of Cadog’s parents, King Gwynllyw of Gwynllŵg and Gwladys, daughter of Brychan, of his schooling, his travels and his miracles, and of the lands and rights of his church, which Lifris set down carefully so that they would never be forgotten.",
    "It is one of the first books anywhere to tell stories of Arthur. In one, Arthur, with Cai and Bedwyr, meets Gwynllyw carrying Gwladys away to be his bride. In another, Arthur comes to Cadog to claim payment for three of his soldiers who had been killed, and the cattle Cadog pays him turn into bundles of fern. Welsh tradition holds that this Arthur, the king of the south-east whom the saints of Gwent and Glamorgan knew, was Athrwys ap Meurig."],
   ["Mab Herewald, Esgob Llandaf yn ail hanner yr unfed ganrif ar ddeg, oedd Lifris. Daeth yn Archddiacon Morgannwg ac yn athro Llancarfan, y fynachlog fawr ym Mro Morgannwg a sefydlodd Sant Cadog bum can mlynedd ynghynt.",
    "Tua 1100 ysgrifennodd Fuchedd Sant Cadog yn Lladin. Mae’n adrodd am rieni Cadog, y Brenin Gwynllyw o Wynllŵg a Gwladys ferch Brychan, am ei addysg, ei deithiau a’i wyrthiau, ac am diroedd a hawliau ei eglwys, a gofnododd Lifris yn ofalus fel na chaent fyth eu hanghofio.",
    "Mae’n un o’r llyfrau cyntaf yn unman i adrodd straeon am Arthur. Yn un ohonynt mae Arthur, gyda Chai a Bedwyr, yn cyfarfod Gwynllyw yn cario Gwladys ymaith i fod yn wraig iddo. Mewn un arall, daw Arthur at Gadog i hawlio iawndal am dri o’i filwyr a laddwyd, ac mae’r gwartheg a rydd Cadog iddo yn troi’n sypiau o redyn. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig oedd yr Arthur hwn, brenin y de-ddwyrain a adwaenai saint Gwent a Morgannwg."]),
 P("ieuan-ap-sulien", "kings", "c. 1072–1137", "Ieuan ap Sulien", "Ieuan ap Sulien", "Poet and artist of Llanbadarn", "Bardd ac arlunydd Llanbadarn", "Ieuan_ap_Sulien", "learning",
   "Son of Bishop Sulien and brother of Rhygyfarch, who decorated the Rhygyfarch Psalter and wrote the Latin poem on his father’s life.",
   "Mab yr Esgob Sulien a brawd Rhygyfarch, a addurnodd Sallwyr Rhygyfarch ac a ysgrifennodd y gerdd Ladin ar fywyd ei dad.",
   ["Ieuan was the second son of Sulien of Llanbadarn Fawr, and like his brothers he spent his life in learning. He was a scribe and an artist: the great knotwork letters of the Rhygyfarch Psalter, made at Llanbadarn about 1080 and now in Trinity College, Dublin, are his work, and he copied and decorated a book of Saint Augustine that is now in Cambridge.",
    "He was a poet as well. His long Latin poem on the life and family of his father tells how Sulien went to study in Scotland and Ireland, came home to teach, and twice became Bishop of St Davids. It is the first life of a Welsh scholar written by his own son.",
    "Ieuan became archpriest of Llanbadarn and died there in 1137. The chronicle of the princes remembers him among the most learned men of the Britons, the last of the family that made Llanbadarn the school of Wales."],
   ["Ieuan oedd ail fab Sulien o Lanbadarn Fawr, ac fel ei frodyr treuliodd ei oes mewn dysg. Roedd yn gopïydd ac yn arlunydd: ei waith ef yw priflythrennau cwlwm mawr Sallwyr Rhygyfarch, a wnaed yn Llanbadarn tua 1080 ac sydd heddiw yng Ngholeg y Drindod, Dulyn, a chopïodd ac addurnodd lyfr gan Sant Awstin sydd heddiw yng Nghaergrawnt.",
    "Roedd yn fardd hefyd. Mae ei gerdd Ladin hir ar fywyd a theulu ei dad yn adrodd sut yr aeth Sulien i astudio yn yr Alban ac Iwerddon, dod adref i ddysgu, a dod ddwywaith yn Esgob Tyddewi. Dyma’r fuchedd gyntaf i ysgolhaig o Gymro a ysgrifennwyd gan ei fab ei hun.",
    "Daeth Ieuan yn archoffeiriad Llanbadarn a bu farw yno yn 1137. Mae cronicl y tywysogion yn ei gofio ymhlith gwŷr dysgedicaf y Brythoniaid, yr olaf o’r teulu a wnaeth Lanbadarn yn ysgol Cymru."]),
 P("caradog-llancarfan", "medieval", "fl. c. 1135", "Caradog of Llancarfan", "Caradog o Lancarfan", "Writer of the lives of the saints", "Awdur bucheddau’r saint", "Caradoc_of_Llancarfan", "learning",
   "Scholar of Llancarfan who wrote the Lives of Gildas and Cadog, and whom Geoffrey of Monmouth named as the historian of the kings of Wales.",
   "Ysgolhaig o Lancarfan a ysgrifennodd Fucheddau Gildas a Chadog, ac a enwodd Sieffre o Fynwy yn hanesydd brenhinoedd Cymru.",
   ["Caradog lived and wrote at Llancarfan, Cadog’s monastery in the Vale of Glamorgan, in the first half of the twelfth century. He wrote in Latin the Life of Gildas and a Life of Saint Cadog, and at the end of the Life of Gildas he names himself in a short verse.",
    "His Life of Gildas holds a famous story of Arthur. Melwas, king of the Summer Country, had carried off Gwenhwyfar to Glastonbury, and Arthur came with the armies of Cornwall and Dyfnaint to win her back. Gildas and the abbot of Glastonbury went between the two kings and made peace, and Gwenhwyfar was given back to Arthur.",
    "Geoffrey of Monmouth, who knew him, ended his History of the Kings of Britain by leaving the story of the later kings of Wales to ‘my contemporary Caradog of Llancarfan’. For that reason Welsh tradition long held that Caradog had written the first part of the chronicle of the princes, Brut y Tywysogion, and when David Powel printed the history of Wales in 1584 he named Caradog as its first author."],
   ["Bu Caradog yn byw ac yn ysgrifennu yn Llancarfan, mynachlog Cadog ym Mro Morgannwg, yn hanner cyntaf y ddeuddegfed ganrif. Ysgrifennodd yn Lladin Fuchedd Gildas a Buchedd Sant Cadog, ac ar ddiwedd Buchedd Gildas mae’n enwi ei hun mewn pennill byr.",
    "Mae ei Fuchedd Gildas yn cadw stori enwog am Arthur. Roedd Melwas, brenin Gwlad yr Haf, wedi cipio Gwenhwyfar i Ynys Wydrin, a daeth Arthur â byddinoedd Cernyw a Dyfnaint i’w hennill yn ôl. Aeth Gildas ac abad Ynys Wydrin rhwng y ddau frenin a gwneud heddwch, a rhoddwyd Gwenhwyfar yn ôl i Arthur.",
    "Gorffennodd Sieffre o Fynwy, a’i hadwaenai, ei Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain drwy adael hanes brenhinoedd diweddarach Cymru i ‘fy nghyfoeswr Caradog o Lancarfan’. Am hynny daliodd traddodiad Cymru am hir mai Caradog a ysgrifennodd ran gyntaf cronicl y tywysogion, Brut y Tywysogion, a phan argraffodd David Powel hanes Cymru yn 1584 enwodd Garadog yn awdur cyntaf iddo."]),
 P("walter-map", "medieval", "c. 1130–c. 1209", "Walter Map", "Gwallter Map", "Storyteller of the Welsh border", "Storïwr y Gororau", "Walter_Map", "words",
   "A churchman of the Welsh border who called himself a man of the March of Wales and filled his book Courtiers’ Trifles with the tales of the Welsh.",
   "Eglwyswr o’r Gororau a alwai ei hun yn ŵr o Fers Cymru ac a lanwodd ei lyfr Mân Chwedlau’r Llys â straeon y Cymry.",
   ["Walter Map was born about 1130 on the Welsh border, probably in the south-west of Herefordshire, and he was proud of it. ‘I am a marcher of the Welsh,’ he wrote, a man of the borderland, and he claimed Welsh blood. He studied in Paris and rose in the service of King Henry II as a royal clerk and judge. He went to Rome for the king in 1179, became a canon of Hereford and of Lincoln, and in 1196 Archdeacon of Oxford. In 1203 his name was put forward to be Bishop of St Davids.",
    "He was the best talker at the court of Henry II, and his one surviving book, De nugis curialium, Courtiers’ Trifles, is a treasure chest of stories: of kings and monks, ghosts and wonders, and above all of the Welsh. He tells of Gwestin of Gwestiniog, who married a fairy maiden from the lake of Brycheiniog; of Eadric the Wild and his fairy bride; and of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, king of all Wales, and his sayings. Some of the oldest Welsh folk tales were written down for the first time in its pages.",
    "In France a century later, the great prose romances of Lancelot and the Holy Grail were written under his name, ‘Gautier Map’, so that the storyteller of the Welsh border was honoured as the father of the Arthurian romances of Europe. He died about 1209 and is remembered at Hereford Cathedral."],
   ["Ganed Gwallter Map tua 1130 ar y gororau, yn ne-orllewin Swydd Henffordd yn ôl pob tebyg, ac roedd yn falch o hynny. ‘Gŵr y Mers wyf i i’r Cymry,’ ysgrifennodd, gŵr o’r gororau, a hawliai waed Cymreig. Astudiodd ym Mharis a dringodd yng ngwasanaeth y Brenin Harri II yn glerc brenhinol ac yn farnwr. Aeth i Rufain dros y brenin yn 1179, daeth yn ganon Henffordd a Lincoln, ac yn 1196 yn Archddiacon Rhydychen. Yn 1203 cynigiwyd ei enw i fod yn Esgob Tyddewi.",
    "Ef oedd siaradwr gorau llys Harri II, ac mae ei unig lyfr sydd wedi goroesi, De nugis curialium, Mân Chwedlau’r Llys, yn gist drysor o straeon: am frenhinoedd a mynachod, ysbrydion a rhyfeddodau, ac yn bennaf am y Cymry. Mae’n adrodd am Wastin Wastiniog, a briododd forwyn o’r tylwyth teg o lyn Brycheiniog; am Edric Wyllt a’i briodferch o’r tylwyth teg; ac am Ruffudd ap Llywelyn, brenin Cymru gyfan, a’i ddywediadau. Ysgrifennwyd rhai o chwedlau gwerin hynaf Cymru am y tro cyntaf ar ei dudalennau.",
    "Yn Ffrainc ganrif yn ddiweddarach, ysgrifennwyd rhamantau rhyddiaith mawr Lawnslot a’r Greal Sanctaidd dan ei enw, ‘Gautier Map’, fel yr anrhydeddwyd storïwr y gororau yn dad rhamantau Arthuraidd Ewrop. Bu farw tua 1209 ac fe’i cofir yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Henffordd."]),
 P("adam-of-usk", "medieval", "c. 1352–1430", "Adam of Usk", "Adda o Frynbuga", "Chronicler of the age of Glyndŵr", "Croniclydd oes Glyndŵr", "Adam_of_Usk", "learning",
   "Lawyer and chronicler from Usk whose chronicle keeps the words of Owain Glyndŵr’s letters to Scotland and Ireland.",
   "Cyfreithiwr a chroniclydd o Frynbuga y mae ei gronicl yn cadw geiriau llythyrau Owain Glyndŵr at yr Alban ac Iwerddon.",
   ["Adam was born at Usk, Brynbuga, in Gwent about 1352. He studied law at Oxford, became a doctor of law and one of the leading lawyers of his day, and worked at the court of the Archbishop of Canterbury and then at the Pope’s court in Rome.",
    "His Latin chronicle runs from 1377 to 1421 and is full of what he saw himself: the fall of Richard II, Rome, the Pope, and the rising of Owain Glyndŵr. Above all, he copied into it the letter Owain sent in 1401 to Robert III, King of Scots, in which Owain reminds the king that both their peoples are descended from Brutus, and the letter to the lords of Ireland. Without Adam, Owain’s own words would be lost.[[cite:adam-usk]]",
    "He came home to Wales in the years of the rising and lived for a time among Owain’s people before he was pardoned in 1411. He spent his last years at Usk, where he died in 1430. His brass in the church at Usk remembers him in Welsh verse, one of the oldest Welsh inscriptions in Gwent."],
   ["Ganed Adda ym Mrynbuga yng Ngwent tua 1352. Astudiodd y gyfraith yn Rhydychen, daeth yn ddoethur yn y gyfraith ac yn un o gyfreithwyr blaenaf ei ddydd, a bu’n gweithio yn llys Archesgob Caergaint ac yna yn llys y Pab yn Rhufain.",
    "Mae ei gronicl Lladin yn rhedeg o 1377 i 1421 ac yn llawn o’r hyn a welodd ei hun: cwymp Rhisiart II, Rhufain, y Pab, a gwrthryfel Owain Glyndŵr. Yn bennaf oll, copïodd iddo’r llythyr a anfonodd Owain yn 1401 at Robert III, Brenin yr Alban, lle mae Owain yn atgoffa’r brenin fod eu dwy bobl yn disgyn o Frutus, a’r llythyr at arglwyddi Iwerddon. Heb Adda, byddai geiriau Owain ei hun ar goll.[[cite:adam-usk]]",
    "Daeth adref i Gymru ym mlynyddoedd y gwrthryfel a bu’n byw am gyfnod ymhlith pobl Owain cyn cael pardwn yn 1411. Treuliodd ei flynyddoedd olaf ym Mrynbuga, lle bu farw yn 1430. Mae ei blac pres yn eglwys Brynbuga yn ei goffáu mewn mydr Cymraeg, un o’r arysgrifau Cymraeg hynaf yng Ngwent."]),
 P("gutun-owain", "medieval", "fl. 1450–1498", "Gutun Owain", "Gutun Owain", "Poet, scribe and keeper of the chronicles", "Bardd, copïydd a cheidwad y croniclau", "Gutun_Owain", "words",
   "Poet and scribe of the Oswestry border who copied the Welsh chronicles at Basingwerk Abbey and helped trace the British descent of Henry Tudor.",
   "Bardd a chopïydd o gyffiniau Croesoswallt a gopïodd groniclau Cymru yn Abaty Dinas Basing ac a helpodd olrhain tras Frythonig Harri Tudur.",
   ["Gutun Owain came from Dudleston, near Oswestry, and was a pupil of the great poet Dafydd ab Edmwnd. He was a poet of the strict metres, and he wrote cywyddau to the abbots of Basingwerk and Valle Crucis, the great Cistercian houses of north-east Wales, whose libraries he used.",
    "He was above all a keeper of the old learning. He copied in his own clear hand the Welsh history of the kings of Britain and the chronicle of the princes, in the book now known as the Black Book of Basingwerk, along with genealogies, heraldry, medicine, astronomy and the calendar.",
    "Welsh tradition remembers him as one of the learned men who, after Bosworth, traced the descent of King Henry VII back through the princes of Wales to the old kings of the Britons."],
   ["O Ddudlust, ger Croesoswallt, y daeth Gutun Owain, ac roedd yn ddisgybl i’r bardd mawr Dafydd ab Edmwnd. Bardd y mesurau caeth ydoedd, ac ysgrifennodd gywyddau i abadau Dinas Basing a Glyn y Groes, tai mawr y Sistersiaid yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru, y defnyddiodd eu llyfrgelloedd.",
    "Yn bennaf oll roedd yn geidwad yr hen ddysg. Copïodd yn ei law glir ei hun hanes Cymraeg brenhinoedd Prydain a chronicl y tywysogion, yn y llyfr a elwir heddiw yn Llyfr Du Basing, ynghyd ag achau, herodraeth, meddygaeth, seryddiaeth a’r calendr.",
    "Mae traddodiad Cymru yn ei gofio fel un o’r gwŷr dysgedig a olrheiniodd, wedi Bosworth, dras y Brenin Harri VII yn ôl drwy dywysogion Cymru at hen frenhinoedd y Brythoniaid."]),
 P("elis-gruffydd", "tudor", "c. 1490–c. 1552", "Elis Gruffydd", "Elis Gruffydd", "The soldier of Calais", "Milwr Calais", "Elis_Gruffydd", "words",
   "Soldier and chronicler from Flintshire who wrote the history of the world in Welsh, the longest chronicle in the language, and kept the oldest telling of the tale of Taliesin.",
   "Milwr a chroniclydd o Sir y Fflint a ysgrifennodd hanes y byd yn Gymraeg, y cronicl hwyaf yn yr iaith, a chadw’r fersiwn hynaf o chwedl Taliesin.",
   ["Elis Gruffydd was born at Gronant in Flintshire around 1490. He went to London, served Sir Robert Wingfield, and became a soldier in the English garrison at Calais, which is why he is remembered as ‘the soldier of Calais’. He fought in the French wars and saw kings and battles at first hand.",
    "In his spare hours at Calais he wrote, in Welsh, a chronicle of the whole history of the world from the Creation to his own day: some two thousand pages, the longest work of history ever written in Welsh. He drew on the Welsh Bruts and on English and French chronicles, and he wrote down the stories he had heard at home in Wales.",
    "His chronicle keeps the oldest full telling of the tale of Taliesin, of Gwion Bach and Ceridwen’s cauldron, and the tale of the Welshman who found Arthur and his warriors asleep in a cave. Through him, stories that had lived only on the tongues of the people of north Wales were saved."],
   ["Ganed Elis Gruffydd yng Ngronant yn Sir y Fflint tua 1490. Aeth i Lundain, gwasanaethodd Syr Robert Wingfield, a daeth yn filwr yng ngarsiwn Lloegr yn Calais, a dyna pam y’i cofir fel ‘milwr Calais’. Ymladdodd yn y rhyfeloedd yn Ffrainc a gwelodd frenhinoedd a brwydrau â’i lygaid ei hun.",
    "Yn ei oriau hamdden yn Calais ysgrifennodd, yn Gymraeg, gronicl o holl hanes y byd o’r Creu hyd ei ddydd ei hun: tua dwy fil o dudalennau, y gwaith hanes hwyaf a ysgrifennwyd erioed yn Gymraeg. Tynnodd ar Frutiau Cymru ac ar groniclau Saesneg a Ffrangeg, ac ysgrifennodd y straeon a glywsai gartref yng Nghymru.",
    "Mae ei gronicl yn cadw’r fersiwn llawn hynaf o chwedl Taliesin, am Wion Bach a pheir Ceridwen, a chwedl y Cymro a gafodd Arthur a’i ryfelwyr yn cysgu mewn ogof. Drwyddo ef achubwyd straeon a fu’n byw ar dafod pobl y gogledd yn unig."]),
]

